On 2007-09-09, Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> Ohle Claussen wrote:
>> I guess you tagged a space as bad based on original layout, i.e. after some
>> trap filling, there will be fewer "bad" spots?
>
> Yes, it's based on the original layout (but existing boulder locations are
> disregarded). To illustrate in an example (soko2-1)...
>
>    --------------------
>    |XXXXXXXX|XXX|ooooo|
>    |X....-..|.-X|ooooo|
>    |X.|.....|..X|ooooo|
>    |-.|XX-..|.-X|ooooo|
>    |X.X--x.....X|ooooo|
>    |X..|X.X-..X-|ooooo|
>    |X..|..|XXX--|ooooo|
>    |-..|..|----------+|
>    |X......^^^^^^^^^^X|
>    |XXX|XX|------------
>    --------
>
> 'X' are "bad", '^' are "trap", '.' are "cond", and 'o' are "okay".
> 'x' is also "bad" (a correction of the previously posted data).
>
> Traps are mostly (always?) good, since they are immediately filled by
> teleported boulders and if a boulder hits a filled square you can push it
> to the next trap. (Teleport of multiple boulders is another case.)
>
> Of course what's good or bad depends on more factors. So if you have not
> enough boulders left to move then all the 'o' are bad locations. OTOH, the
> spots marked as 'X' may be okay if you have spare boulders and just want
> a boulder out of your way. The really risky and unpredictable spots are
> the '.' where they possibly interfere with other existing boulders.

So the ratio of bad/(spaces-okay) can be taken as a lower bound of the
probability of a teleport not making it possible to use a trapped boulder
for hole filling. This is consistently between around 30% and 40%, and gets
worse when other boulders are present. Confirms my estimation --
teleporting is only useful when you have very few boulders left and don't
mind spending a few charges. Of course, when the boulder ends up in an
"okay" square behind an unfilled trap, it's gone.

